{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00420 - MONDO_grouped_2309_2308",
  "verbalization": "Promazine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Papillary Conjunctivitis through a chain of indications that link it to Atopic Conjunctivitis, which in turn is associated with other drugs such as Alcaftadine, Tromethamine, and Levocabastine. These drugs are all indicated for Papillary Conjunctivitis, suggesting a potential shared mechanism or pathway in the development of the condition. The evidence suggests that Promazine may act through a mechanism that indirectly influences the inflammatory response or ocular physiology, possibly by modulating the immune system or affecting epithelial function, which could contribute to the development of Papillary Conjunctivitis. The consistent presence of Atopic Conjunctivitis as a common intermediate in these pathways supports the hypothesis that Promazine may have a role in the pathophysiology of this condition, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
